.page regex 7 regular expressions as understood by the system tools

# DESCRIPTION
A regular expression describes a set of strings. The system's C library implements two flavours, and the tools use them like this:

    basic (BRE)       grep, sed, expr, find -regex
    extended (ERE)    grep -E, egrep, sed -E, pnote (Alt-R), the pager

The two flavours differ only in which characters are special and which need a backslash. The matcher works on bytes, not on multibyte characters, and finds the leftmost match; of the alternatives it tries them in order and keeps the first that leads to a match, which is not always the longest (`a|ab` matches only `a` in `ab`). Repetition is greedy. A pattern that would make the matcher run for an absurd number of steps is given up as "no match".

# THE COMMON PARTS
@ c
    An ordinary character matches itself.
@ .
    Any single character.
@ [abc], [a-z], [^abc]
    A bracket expression: any one of the characters or ranges listed, or with `^` first, any one that is not listed. A `]` placed first is a member of the set. Inside the brackets the character classes `[:alpha:]`, `[:digit:]`, `[:alnum:]`, `[:upper:]`, `[:lower:]`, `[:space:]`, `[:blank:]`, `[:punct:]`, `[:print:]`, `[:graph:]`, `[:cntrl:]` and `[:xdigit:]` may be used: `[[:digit:]_]`.
@ ^
    The beginning of the line (basic: only at the start of the pattern or of a group or alternative).
@ $
    The end of the line (basic: only at the end of the pattern or of a group or alternative).
@ *
    Zero or more of the preceding item. In a basic expression a `*` at the very start is an ordinary character.
@ \\.  \\*  \\[  \\\\
    A backslash makes the next special character ordinary.

These escapes are available in both flavours:

    \n  \t        newline and tab
    \d  \D        a digit; anything but a digit
    \w  \W        a word character (letter, digit, _); its opposite
    \s  \S        white space; its opposite
    \b  \B        a word boundary; anywhere that is not one
    \<  \>        the beginning and the end of a word
    \1 ... \9     what the first ... ninth group matched, again

# BASIC EXPRESSIONS
Groups, repetition counts and alternation need a backslash:

    \( \)         group, and remember what it matched
    \{n\}         exactly n repetitions
    \{n,\}        at least n repetitions
    \{n,m\}       between n and m repetitions
    \+  \?        one or more; zero or one
    \|            alternation

# EXTENDED EXPRESSIONS
The same operators are written without the backslash:

    ( )           group
    {n}  {n,}     exactly n; at least n repetitions
    {n,m}         between n and m repetitions
    +  ?          one or more; zero or one
    |             alternation

A `{` that does not begin a valid count is an ordinary character.

# EXAMPLES
    grep '^reimu:' /Hakurei/etc/passwd
    grep -E '(cat|dog)s?' pets.txt
    sed 's/\(.*\)@\(.*\)/\2 at \1/' addresses.txt
    sed -E 's/([0-9]+)-([0-9]+)/\2-\1/' ranges.txt
    expr "hello world" : '\(h.*o\)'
    find /Hakurei -regex '.*\.conf'

# SEE ALSO
grep(1), sed(1), expr(1), find(1), glob(7), pnote(1)
